Molecular dynamics (MD) simulations are used to simulate the behavior of molecular systems. MD simulations for large molecular systems require significant computational resources to achieve good performance. Existing MD libraries, such as LAMMPS, use MPI to run MD simulations in distributed settings. Communication costs can be a significant bottleneck in MD simulations, however, as MPI processes communicate with each other at every timestep in the simulation. We introduce StencilMD, an algorithm that reduces communication costs in MD simulations. By adapting trapezoidal decompositions from cache-efficient stencil computations, StencilMD decomposes the space-time of an MD simulation into 4-dimensional regions in order to reduce the frequency of communication and synchronization between processes and enable overlapping computation and communication. We implemented StencilMD within LAMMPS and evaluated its performance in practice. Across five standard benchmark MD simulations of varying complexities, StencilMD substantially reduces communication overhead and achieves a 1.4 × –1.9 × speedup compared to LAMMPS on 768 CPU cores.